WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.599 --> 00:00:12.039
Hello everyone. Welcome to the LangFocus channel 
and my name is Paul

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:15.120
Today, I'm going to talk about the Maltese language.

00:00:15.199 --> 00:00:20.059
Maltese is a unique and interesting language 
because it is a Semitic language with a twist.

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:24.240
It is the only Semitic language that is
 an official language of the European Union.

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:27.379
It is spoken by around 520 thousand people,

00:00:27.379 --> 00:00:31.739
mostly on the island nation of Malta
which is an island in the  Mediterranean Sea,

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:35.960
not far from Sicily and not far 
from Tunisia and Libya in North Africa.

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:39.780
Some people claim that Maltese is a descendant 
of the ancient Phoenician language,

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:42.160
but actually there's no linguistic evidence for this.

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:47.600
Maltese is a descendant of a dialect of Arabic, 
called Siculo-Arabic or Sicilian Arabic.

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:53.120
But it has diverged quite a lot from Arabic over the last
1000 years or so, and has now become its own language.

00:00:53.259 --> 00:00:58.639
The syntax and grammar of Maltese remains Arabic 
and about a third of the vocabulary is still Arabic.

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:03.580
And that includes most of the grammatical function 
words as well as a lot of the most basic vocabulary.

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:06.959
About half of the vocabulary is from Italian or Sicilian

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:10.460
and the remaining vocabulary is
 mostly from English and French.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:15.400
It's still considered a Semitic language because of 
its Arabic origin and because of its structure. But...

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:19.580
if you take into account all of the foreign loan words, 
especially the romance loanwords,

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:21.379
then it's essentially a hybrid language.

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:23.720
Kind of like a Semitic-Romance hybrid.

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:29.000
So how did this interesting mix of influences 
come together to create Maltese?

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:31.099
Well, it's because of its history, of course.

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:36.640
The Fatimid Caliphate which covered much of North Africa
conquered Sicily at the end of the 9th century

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:40.540
and brought their Maghrebi or North African dialect 
of Arabic with them.

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:46.240
Then, in 1049 CE, Arabic-speaking Muslim settlers 
from Sicily arrived in Malta,

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:50.520
which is only about 200 kilometers away,
and began to establish communities there.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:55.880
In the year 1091 CE, the Normans, who were 
Christians from Normandy and present-day France,

00:01:56.080 --> 00:02:00.880
had defeated the Arabs and taken hold of Sicily
and then began taking hold of Malta too.

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:06.200
Under Norman rule, more settlers from Sicily began 
arriving in Malta and bringing their language with them.

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:11.719
Even though the Normans were Christian, the population 
of Sicily was still a mixture of Christians and Muslims

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:14.420
who spoke Arabic alongside the Sicilian language.

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:21.160
After the decline of Norman rule, the new regime in Sicily
expelled all of the Muslims from Sicily and from Malta as well.

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:28.260
This was a very significant event,
 because it essentially cut off the Siculo-Arabic dialect
from the Muslim Arabic-speaking world.

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:31.379
And it also cut it off from the classical Arabic language.

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:38.240
In other Arabic-speaking lands, the Quran remained 
a conservative force that prevented the dialects 
from changing too much overtime.

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:42.220
But, in Malta, that conservative force
was no longer connected to the dialect.

00:02:42.319 --> 00:02:46.519
So the dialect was free to drift off in its own direction 
and become a new language.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:51.600
Sicuro-Arabic died out in Sicily 
but it continued to be used and evolve in Malta.

00:02:51.819 --> 00:02:54.979
Malta remained part of the kingdom of Sicily 
for several hundred years

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:58.180
which brought lots of sicilian vocabulary into the language.

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:02.960
The island was then controlled by a string of rulers 
who influenced the developing Maltese language.

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:07.780
One of the most significant regimes to rule Malta 
was the Knights Hospitaller of St. John,

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:12.560
a powerful order of knights that assisted and protected 
Christian pilgrims on their way to the Holy Land.

00:03:12.639 --> 00:03:19.179
They were given the island of Malta in 1530 
as an outpost to help prevent an Ottoman invasion 
of Rome from the South.

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:24.940
They made Italian the official language of the island
and it remained the official language until 1934.

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:28.960
This resulted in a large amount of Italian vocabulary
entering the language.

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:32.480
There was also a period of two years
when Malta was under French rule.

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:36.220
And just from those two years, quite a bit of 
French vocabulary entered the language too.

00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:40.400
And the island was a British protectorate 
from 1802 to 1956.

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:44.120
And, of course, that led to the adoption 
of lots of English words into Maltese.

00:03:44.219 --> 00:03:49.859
So Maltese is essentially an Arabic dialect that 
has become Latinized and to some extent Anglicized.

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:52.340
So how similar is it to Arabic today?

00:03:52.479 --> 00:03:58.619
Well, if you know Arabic and you try to read 
some Maltese, you will quickly realize 
that it's basically Arabic in structure,

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:02.620
but you will also see a lot of foreign vocabulary, 
mostly Romance vocabulary.

00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:07.980
It is most similar to the Maghreb dialect of Arabic, 
meaning the dialects of Morocco, Algeria and Tunisia,

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:09.720
especially the Tunisian dialect.

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:13.320
A lot of Tunisians claim that they can
quite easily understand Maltese.

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:17.160
That could be because Tunisian Arabic itself 
has quite a few Italian loan words.

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:21.379
And I also understand that a lot of Tunisians 
can understand Standard Italian.

00:04:21.379 --> 00:04:26.019
So, if they speak Tunisian Arabic and Italian, 
then they can quite easily understand Maltese.

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:30.220
Lots of people say that if you know an Arabic dialect 
quite well and if you also know Italian,

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:32.620
then reading Maltese won't be much of a problem

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:36.519
and, with some adjustment, you can also learn 
to understand it fairly easily.

00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:40.720
That doesn't mean you could speak proper Maltese 
but maybe you could use your Arabic and your Italian

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:43.560
and sort of pack a conversation 
in a new pidgin language.

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:46.760
So let's look at a couple example sentences of Maltese.

00:04:47.079 --> 00:04:50.319
So the sentence here is an example 
of one that's mostly Arabic.

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:53.920
By the way, I've never learned to speak  Maltese, 
so forgive my pronunciation.

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:05.480
Now, I understood this sentence almost perfectly 
and it's basically all Arabic.

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:07.819
The first word "...", that's "Japan".

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:09.439
"...", that's "has"

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:11.100
"...", that's "the tenth"

00:05:11.320 --> 00:05:13.000
"...", that's "the biggest"

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:17.180
"...", that's not Arabic. That's... 
That looks like an Italian loan word to me.

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:19.680
"...", so that's "in the world"

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:22.000
"...", that means with "more than"

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:24.860
And then, there's the Arabic number (126)

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:27.700
And then, there's "miljun",  which is "million" in Arabic too.

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:31.839
And then, "persuna" which is not Arabic, 
but I guess that's from Italian or  Sicilian.

00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:35.720
But sometimes, you see sentences
with a lot more loan words, particularly Italian.

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:36.700
Here's another sentence.

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:45.720
So this sentence means: "Migration reform
must include the principle of solidarity."

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:47.180
This one has a lot of loan words.

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:50.240
"Riforma", that's not an Arabic word.
That's a loan word.

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:55.080
"Fil-migrazzjoni" : "Fil", that part is Arabic 
but "migrazzjoni", that is a loan word.

00:05:55.360 --> 00:05:59.340
"ghandha", that part is interesting 
because this is an Arabic word  that means "has"

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:02.320
but, in this case, 
it's being used  to mean "has to" or "must".

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:04.079
And it's not used that way in Arabic.

00:06:04.079 --> 00:06:07.719
So the function of this word has become
somewhat adapted in the Maltese language.

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:14.980
The next word "tinkludi', that's "include"
but that the "t" links back to the previous vowel, 
that's an element of Arabic syntax.

00:06:15.160 --> 00:06:20.120
The next word "...", so that's obviously "principle" 
but that's not an Arabic word, that's a loan word.

00:06:21.839 --> 00:06:29.979
Again that's the Arabic definite article and the "t" 
is linking back to the previous vowel and "solidarieta", 
that's "solidarity" and that's not an Arabic word either.

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:32.880
So you can see a lot of sentences 
like this in Maltese, often.

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:37.199
If you know Arabic, you'll recognize the structure 
but then, a lot of the words are not Arabic.

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:43.600
For me personally, I know that, with my past study 
of Arabic and with my fluency in English 
and my recent basic knowledge of Italian,

00:06:43.780 --> 00:06:48.240
I can take a stab at reading Maltese and I can 
understand the fair bit of it... But not all of it!

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:54.700
When I hear spoken Maltese, I don't really understand it 
but I can pick out some individual Arabic and Italian 
and English words

00:06:54.920 --> 00:06:58.920
But I imagine that, as my Italian improves,
I'll be able to understand a bit more of it.

00:06:59.060 --> 00:07:07.100
And if I decided to review my Arabic in depth and started 
to learn a Maghrebi dialect, then I think I would be able 
to understand a lot more Maltese as well.

00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:13.240
Maltese is not a very widely spoken language,
so not that many people learn it and there aren't 
that many materials out there for learners.

00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:19.639
But, for someone who's learning Arabic or learning 
Italian, then it might be fun to dabble in 
some Maltese and see what you can make of it.

00:07:19.780 --> 00:07:21.519
Thank you for watching the LangFocus channel.

